  const dictionary=toon?.querySelector('.dictionary');
  if(dictionary&&!toon.querySelector('.toon-places'))dictionary.insertAdjacentHTML('beforebegin',`<div class="toon-places"><article class="toon-place"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Newcastle-upon-Tyne-bridges-and-skyline_cropped.jpg/1280px-Newcastle-upon-Tyne-bridges-and-skyline_cropped.jpg" alt="Newcastle skyline and bridges across the River Tyne" loading="lazy"><div><small>THE CITY</small><h3>Newcastle upon Tyne</h3><p>The regional capital on the north bank of the Tyne: bridges, steep streets, nightlife, football and a fierce local identity. “The Toon” can mean Newcastle itself or Newcastle United, depending on the sentence.</p><a href="https://www.newcastlegateshead.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore Newcastle ↗</a></div></article><article class="toon-place"><img src="https://d3pjx2d5l0uj4a.cloudfront.net/dronescenephotos/cbfa652e-9c1e-4682-94ab-2ea00df9a5da_2022111017.jpg" alt="Fishing boats and buildings at North Shields Fish Quay" loading="lazy"><div><small>SAM’S HOMETOWN</small><h3>North Shields</h3><p>Sam grew up downriver at North Shields, where the Tyne meets the North Sea. Its Fish Quay grew from a medieval fishing settlement and remains central to the town’s working maritime character—the pubs, streets and people behind many of his stories.</p><a href="https://sitelines.newcastle.gov.uk/SMR/11867" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Fish Quay history ↗</a></div></article><article class="toon-place"><img src="https://contentfulproxy.stadion.io/9ec6988xevcz/6rLgNey9HtOXzhzwjynGJ0/65a62d5f4af806ab898ab5ba9aa4b2e5/20181008-st-james-park-exterior.jpg?f=face&amp;fit=fill&amp;fm=webp&amp;h=900&amp;w=1500" alt="Exterior of Newcastle United’s St James’ Park stadium" loading="lazy"><div><small>THE HOME GROUND</small><h3>St James’ Park</h3><p>Football has been played here since 1880 and Newcastle United made it home in 1892. The city-centre stadium now holds more than 52,000—and Sam’s three 2025 hometown concerts turned it into one enormous Geordie choir.</p><a href="https://www.newcastleunited.com/en/history-of-our-home" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Stadium history ↗</a></div></article></div>`);
